\subsection*{Euclidean Algorithm}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faLightbulb\ \textbf{The Euclidean Algorithm}}

The Euclidean Algorithm is an efficient method to compute $\gcd(a,b)$ based on the following principle:

If $a = bq + r$, then $\gcd(a,b) = \gcd(b,r)$

\vspace{0.4cm}

Find $\gcd(252, 105)$ using the Euclidean Algorithm.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

Apply Division Algorithm repeatedly:

\begin{align*}
252 &= 105 \times 2 + 42 & \implies \gcd(252, 105) &= \gcd(105, 42)\\
105 &= 42 \times 2 + 21 & \implies \gcd(105, 42) &= \gcd(42, 21)\\
42 &= 21 \times 2 + 0 & \implies \gcd(42, 21) &= \gcd(21, 0) = 21
\end{align*}

Therefore: $\boxed{\gcd(252, 105) = 21}$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.4cm}

Find $\gcd(256, 1170)$ and express it as a linear combination $256x + 1170y$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{Step 1: Apply Euclidean Algorithm}

\begin{align*}
1170 &= 256 \times 4 + 146\\
256 &= 146 \times 1 + 110\\
146 &= 110 \times 1 + 36\\
110 &= 36 \times 3 + 2\\
36 &= 2 \times 18 + 0
\end{align*}

Therefore: $\gcd(256, 1170) = 2$

\textbf{Step 2: Back Substitution (Extended Euclidean Algorithm)}

\begin{align*}
2 &= 110 - 36 \times 3\\
&= 110 - (146 - 110 \times 1) \times 3\\
&= 110 - 146 \times 3 + 110 \times 3\\
&= 110 \times 4 - 146 \times 3\\
&= (256 - 146 \times 1) \times 4 - 146 \times 3\\
&= 256 \times 4 - 146 \times 4 - 146 \times 3\\
&= 256 \times 4 - 146 \times 7\\
&= 256 \times 4 - (1170 - 256 \times 4) \times 7\\
&= 256 \times 4 - 1170 \times 7 + 256 \times 28\\
&= 256 \times 32 - 1170 \times 7
\end{align*}

Therefore: $\boxed{\gcd(256, 1170) = 2 = 256(32) + 1170(-7)}$

\textbf{Verification:} $256 \times 32 + 1170 \times (-7) = 8192 - 8190 = 2$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

