For a positive integer $d$, $d = \gcd(a,b)$ if and only if:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*), leftmargin=*]
    \item $d | a$ and $d | b$
    \item Whenever $c | a$ and $c | b$, then $c | d$
\end{enumerate}

\vspace{0.4cm}

\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Proof:}}

\textbf{Forward Direction ($\Rightarrow$):} Assume $d = \gcd(a,b)$

By definition of GCD:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $d | a$ and $d | b$ (property ii of GCD definition) \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
    \item If $c | a$ and $c | b$, then $c | d$ (property iii of GCD definition) \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Backward Direction ($\Leftarrow$):} Assume conditions (a) and (b) hold

Let $g = \gcd(a,b)$.

Since $g | a$ and $g | b$, by condition (b): $g | d$ \quad $\cdots (1)$

Since $d | a$ and $d | b$, and $g$ is the GCD, by property (iii): $d | g$ \quad $\cdots (2)$

From (1) and (2): $g | d$ and $d | g$

Since both are positive: $g = d$

Therefore, $d = \gcd(a,b)$. \qed

