\vspace{0.4cm}

Express $\gcd(198, 288, 512) = 2$ as a linear combination of 198, 288, and 512.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

From Example 41, we have $\gcd(198, 288, 512) = 2$.

\textbf{Step 1: Express} $\gcd(18, 512) = 2$

Back-substitution from Euclidean Algorithm:
\begin{align*}
2 &= 18 - 8 \times 2\\
&= 18 - (512 - 18 \times 28) \times 2\\
&= 18 - 512 \times 2 + 18 \times 56\\
&= 18 \times 57 - 512 \times 2
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 2: Express} $\gcd(198, 288) = 18$

Back-substitution:
\begin{align*}
18 &= 198 - 90 \times 2\\
&= 198 - (288 - 198 \times 1) \times 2\\
&= 198 - 288 \times 2 + 198 \times 2\\
&= 198 \times 3 - 288 \times 2
\end{align*}

\textbf{Step 3: Combine both results}

Substitute $18 = 198 \times 3 - 288 \times 2$ into $2 = 18 \times 57 - 512 \times 2$:
\begin{align*}
2 &= (198 \times 3 - 288 \times 2) \times 57 - 512 \times 2\\
&= 198 \times 171 - 288 \times 114 - 512 \times 2\\
&= 198 \times 171 + 288 \times (-114) + 512 \times (-2)
\end{align*}

Therefore: $\boxed{2 = 198(171) + 288(-114) + 512(-2)}$

\textbf{Verification:}
\[
198 \times 171 + 288 \times (-114) + 512 \times (-2) = 33858 - 32832 - 1024 = 2
\]
\textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

